cessarily as part of a  distributed system  once you have some ideas around your  bounded contexts  the next thing is to define the  communication between  the different bounded contexts with the  subset shown on the slide  we know the purchasing bounding context  has to get information from the product  bounded context  and i gotta stop saying bounded context  because it's just killing me  so the customer gets information from  the order and geography  order gets information from customer and  product  why because it makes sense uh they have  a relationship between them what does  purchasing do  if it doesn't relate to products or what  is what are orders without products  so as you build out these communication  streams pay attention to the three  important details about the  communications  what each of the context needs to know  why they need to know it  and when they need to know it because  understanding those key bits will help  us design our system  so i pulled out the communications  between product and purchasing  i use this is the example because of how  it shows how domains could be different  uh for different companies the way that  this company defined  um their business is that the product is  the most  important thing and then they will find  out the best place to buy the product  to purchase it many other companies that  i've worked with have a different  approach  where the purchasing domain actually  provides the the product to the products  domain  so the purchasing domain is actually  responsible for creating the product  and then providing that to the product  domain this one is different it takes  the other approach the product  bounded context is responsible for  creating the product  and then allowing that to be sold  through the purchasing bound context  so in this particular case the product  bounded context kind of  um just lets the purchase bounding  context by sending this  pieces of information in the message  so here's how this relationship in the  current system  is currently defined in the database uh  the half with the  with the color is the purchasing schema  the right side is the product schema  there's just a single foreign key  relationship from the purchase order  details table in the purchasing schema  to the product table in the product  schema if you take a look at the product  table  you'll see that it has three tables  directly related to it with a couple of  those related to other tables  this means that a fully instantiated  object graph of a product is going to be  pretty gnarly  and it's hard to imagine that the  purchasing systems needs all of that  information  and it doesn't so while it needs a few  more pieces of information that  we showed here it's only a small  fraction of the overall object graph  basically so that the purchasing agent  can fill out the purchasing order  but it's been identified that's what the  context needs and that's why it needs it  the last question is when in this case  does a purchasing context need strong  consistency or does eventual consistency  work  what kind of lag can your system  tolerate between  when the product context knows about  when a newer change  product happens and when the purchasing  context knows about it  the more tolerance you have the more  eventually consistency  the more eventual consistency is  appropriate  so back to how to plan your refactor  so we talked about describing context  and defining communications what why and  when  the next step is determining the most  critical core area of your application  and to find it in detail  if you remember back to the earlier  slide on ddd  it used the phrase a way of focusing on  core areas for  design consideration and emphasis that's  what we do now  determine those core areas some of this  will be  at the infrastructure level such as  defining sending and receiving messages  others will be more domain level  as you go into your refactor there are a  couple of items you have to do  as you as part of your planning process  the first and most important is to  define your critical area  um this most likely will include several  bounded contexts  this could also be spread across  multiple systems and once you've  identified those critical areas  define the communications between each  part  and then start breaking that chain of  dependencies  remember this process  and the communication schema that looked  like this  would an approach like this make more  sense assuming that eventual consistency  is okay with the business need  this could mean solving this problem the  user website places an order  the website bounded context says  relevant information about that order  it then sends an order placed message  orders and products are both listening  for order place messages  the order takes that message saves all  of the information locally  and does whatever other business  activities need to happen  the product also takes that message but  since all it really cares about is the  product id and quantity that was ordered  it just takes those pieces of  information and decrements the quantity  of available product  and if evolution of the business means  that some other domain is identified and  added to the ubiquitous language that  cares about an order being placed  then you just add that in as well and  you don't affect any of the already  existing code  none of that has to be changed so  rolling in that new functionality  is pretty much as low risk as you can  get  unlike what you would have to do if  you're going to make that change in the  current version  say adding in a new step into the  process that the web has to perform  and since the change is happening on the  website it has the potential of  affecting  all of the other steps of the process  that are happening on the website  so the risk of that change is much  higher than using the new design  we know that the website calls multiple  services as part of its processing  such as the order user and payment so  let's talk about those two highlighted  systems and how they should change  so this is what our goal system looks  like the website will pass information  along to the website service  the website service will persist that  information that the website thinks is  irrelevant  and then queue up a message containing  that data the order service  rather than being directly called by the  big old web app  is now listening to the message queue  for messages that fit the appropriate  criteria  so we're adding two new constructs here  the website service  and it's attached storage in this case  amazon aurora and the message queue  we're changing the big old web  application by removing all of the  business process that it was doing and  moving that into the  processing into the website service  we're changing the order service so that  it listens to the message queue for  order created messages  so the work that needs to be done  refactor the website to remove business  process  create the new website service to do  website domain work  and refactor order service uh to listen  to message queue  so this is pretty much what the website  looks now there's a a bunch of  client-side logic in that hippie  language javascript there's a controller  layer that accepts the user input  it returns the ui elements and there's a  business logic layer that does all of  the processing and orchestration  that's what we're going to move so  ideally  a lot of this would be easy to move  because we already have an abstraction  layer between the controller and the  business logic  i don't know if i would necessarily call  it a repository but other some kind of  interface layer a service layer or  something like that if you do then it's  pretty easy to replace the current  implementation  with a layer that calls the website  where this business logic will move to  if there isn't however we did go over a  refactor pattern that would help with  that  the branch by abs