ally you wouldn't have to change  the api at all that the endpoints for  creating the order still exist or  okay you know especially if there's  other systems that maybe call that  endpoint  that's not something that's happening in  our case here because of the big  monolithic app  but in one of those systems where there  may be multiple applications that are  doing it  you could just keep that api there until  you've had a chance to refactor those  other systems  we'll also need to add a messaging layer  to process any outbound messages if  we're going to be setting anything  um and then we would need to refactor  the business logic layer  so if we remember this approach you'll  see that the order service  did the processing to update the product  inventory and to send the order  confirmation email  but it doesn't have to do that um  because those services will already know  about them  because they're smart that way because  you made the same types of changes to  them  this work seems intimidating there's a  lot of changes to go through  it'll be frustrating it'll take you way  longer than you expect  however it's doable i've done this  several times myself  and as long as you concentrate on  understanding your domain  building your ubiquitous language  differentiating your bounded context  become clear  and in my mind that's the hardest part  finding the communication between these  bounded contexts as well as the business  rules  you know within the bounded context  helps you understand the appropriateness  of specific implementation approaches  such as  cqrs messaging and eventual consistency  within  each individual bounded constant or  context  after understanding all of that  it really seems that writing the code is  simply an  implementation detail  i know we try to cover a lot of things  in a short period i appreciate you  sticking with me  especially since we couldn't get as deep  into the weeds as i imagined some of you  were hoping  some of those earlie